<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William McDougall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William McDougall was a Canadian politician and Father of Confederation who, as a federal minister, negotiated the transfer of Rupert's Land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Appointed lieutenant governor of the North-West Territories in 1869, he was turned back by armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> at the border of the Red River settlement and never took office. His premature proclamation of Canadian authority damaged his career.</p></div>
